{{nihongo foot|'''Rei Ayanami'''|綾波 レイ|Ayanami Rei|{{ipa|ja|ajaꜜnamʲi ɾeː|ipa}}|lead=yes|group=lower-alpha}} is a character from the ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion]]'' anime series and [[Neon Genesis Evangelion (franchise)|its eponymous franchise]], created by the anime studio [[Gainax]]. Rei is an introverted girl who pilots [[Evangelion (mecha)#Evangelion Unit-00|Evangelion Unit-00]], one of the giant [[mecha]] known as Evangelions, and bears the designation First Child. At the beginning of the series, Rei is a mysterious figure whose unusual behavior puzzles her peers. As the series progresses, she becomes more involved with those around her, particularly her classmate and fellow Evangelion pilot, [[Shinji Ikari]]. The series eventually reveals that she is a clone of Shinji's mother, [[Yui Ikari]], and [[Angels (Neon Genesis Evangelion)#Lilith|Lilith]], an entity known as an [[Angels (Neon Genesis Evangelion)|Angel]]. Rei also appears in the [[List of Neon Genesis Evangelion films|franchise's animated feature films]] and related media, including [[List of Neon Genesis Evangelion video games|video games]], the original net animation ''[[Petit Eva: Evangelion@School]]'', the ''[[Rebuild of Evangelion]]'' films, and [[Neon Genesis Evangelion (manga)|Yoshiyuki Sadamoto's manga adaptation]].

[[Hideaki Anno]], director of the animated series, conceived Rei as a representation of his [[unconscious mind]]. His readings in psychology, particularly [[Freud]]ian [[psychoanalysis]] and the theory of the [[Oedipus complex]], also influenced his conception of the character. Character designer Yoshiyuki Sadamoto drew inspiration from [[Paul Gallico]]'s ''[[The Snow Goose (novella)|The Snow Goose]]''; Rei's blue hair reuses a design element from the protagonist of Gainax's unproduced animated film [[Aoki Uru|{{Transliteration|ja|Aoki Uru}}]]. [[Megumi Hayashibara]] voices Rei in Japanese, and [[Amanda Winn-Lee]], [[Brina Palencia]], and Ryan Bartley voice her in English.

Rei has received generally positive responses from viewers and critics. The character became popular within the [[otaku]] community and inspired homages among ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' fans. Reviewers have praised Rei's mysterious aura, her role in the story, and her character development; other critics have described her as lacking personality and being trite. Rei has also inspired a range of merchandise, including figures and accessories, and has been featured in promotional collaborations between ''Evangelion'' and organizations such as the [[Aonami Line]] and the [[Japan National Tourism Organization]]. Her popularity also influenced the music and fashion industries. Critics have attributed her success to a combination of [[Moe (slang)|{{Transliteration|ja|moe}}]], or affectionate, traits that appealed to anime fans and influenced subsequent female anime characters.

==Creation and conception==
===Original concept and influences===
[[File:Rei Ayanami Early Design Der Mond.jpeg|thumb|left|upright=0.5|alt=Concept art of Rei Ayanami|Sadamoto originally designed Rei with dark hair and eyes; he later introduced her blue hair and bandages.]]

[[Hideaki Anno]], director of the anime ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion]]'', chose Ayanami's surname after the Japanese destroyer {{ship|Japanese destroyer|Ayanami|1929|2}}, following the naval naming pattern used for [[List of Neon Genesis Evangelion characters|other characters in the series]].{{sfn|Kuon|2014|p=112}}{{sfn|Tokumu Kikan Chōsa Project Team|2007|p=53}} He took her first name from a character in the anime and manga series ''[[Sailor Moon]]'', [[Rei Hino]], also known as [[Sailor Mars]].<ref name=Essay>{{cite web|last=Anno|first=Hideaki|author-link=Hideaki Anno|url=http://www.gainax.co.jp/hills/anno/essay1.html|title=Essay|date=November 2, 2000|script-website=ja:庵野秀明公式サイト|trans-website=Hideaki Anno Official Site|publisher=[[Gainax]]|access-date=July 5, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070220135947/http://www.gainax.co.jp/hills/anno/essay1.html|archive-date=February 20, 2007|language=ja|url-status=deviated}}</ref> In Japanese, ''Rei'' can mean "zero".{{sfn|Bryce|Cheung|Gutierrez|2010|p=284}}{{efn|This reading applies to the [[kanji]] {{nihongo|{{Transliteration|ja|Rei}}|零}}.<ref name=Essay/>{{sfn|Tokumu Kikan Chōsa Project Team|2007|p=184}}}} According to [[Patrick Drazen]], the name may pun on her role as the pilot of [[Evangelion (mecha)#Evangelion Unit-00|Evangelion Unit-00]].{{sfn|Drazen|2014|p=294}} Critic [[Hiroki Azuma]] suggested that the name may instead derive from Zero, the protagonist of [[Ryū Murakami]]'s 1987 novel ''Ai to Gensō no Fascism''.<ref name="Woznicki"/>

Anno asked character designer [[Yoshiyuki Sadamoto]] to design Rei as a depressed and reticent girl.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=163–164}} The song {{nihongo foot|"{{Transliteration|ja|Doko e demo ikeru kitte}}"|何処へでも行ける切手|group=lower-alpha}} by the band [[Kinniku Shōjo Tai|{{Transliteration|ja|Kinniku Shōjo Tai}}]]<ref>{{Cite web|last=Orita|first=Makadamia|date=May 11, 2023|script-title=ja:"エヴァンゲリオン"は"セーラームーン"なくしては誕生しなかった? 90年代に社会現象を起こした大ヒット作の深〜い関係|trans-title=Could ''Evangelion'' Not Have Been Born without ''Sailor Moon''? Big Hit's Deep Relationships Started Social Phenomenon in the 90s|url=https://futaman.futabanet.jp/articles/-/124142|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260122034103/https://futaman.futabanet.jp/articles/-/124142|archive-date=January 22, 2026|access-date=May 30, 2026|script-website=ja:ふたまん+|trans-website=Futaman+|language=ja}}</ref> inspired Sadamoto, particularly the line {{nihongo foot|"{{Transliteration|ja|hotai de masshiro na shojo}}",|包帯で真っ白な少女||{{literal translation|the girl white with bandages}}|group=lower-alpha}} which prompted him to draw Rei.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|p=164}} The band also released a song named {{nihongo foot|"{{Transliteration|ja|Fumimi no kodomo}}",|福耳の子供|group=lower-alpha}} which features a female monologue; Sadamoto imagined Rei with a similar voice.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=164–165}} Ukina, a character from Sadamoto's previous work ''Koto'', served as Rei's model, and Sadamoto gave Rei a messy, layered bob haircut.<ref name=sadamoto>{{harvnb|Sadamoto|2012|pp=346–348}}. "My Thoughts at the Moment"</ref> Sadamoto also drew inspiration from ''[[The Snow Goose (novella)|The Snow Goose]]'', a novella by [[Paul Gallico]] that describes a painting of its protagonist as a thin, pale girl in an empty room.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=164–165}}

Anno wanted a melancholic<ref>{{Cite web|last=Tei|first=Andrew|date=June 22, 2003|title=Fanime Con 2003 (Sadamoto panel)|url=http://Animeondvd.com/press/conventions/2003/fanime2003.php|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040815044833/http://animeondvd.com/press/conventions/2003/fanime2003.php|archive-date=August 15, 2004|access-date=July 27, 2021|website=Anime on DVD}}</ref> character with short hair, so Sadamoto originally designed Rei with dark eyes and hair. To distinguish her from the other female protagonist, [[Asuka Langley Soryu]], he gave Rei eye and hair colors that contrasted with Asuka's.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.animeclick.it/news/36125-milano-manga-festival-reportage-dei-sadamoto-days|title=Milano Manga Festival: Reportage dei Sadamoto Days|date=July 11, 2013|trans-title=Milano Manga Festival: Report of Sadamoto Days|website=[[AnimeClick.it]]|access-date=July 5, 2014|language=it|archive-date=July 12, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140712051527/http://www.animeclick.it/news/36125-milano-manga-festival-reportage-dei-sadamoto-days|url-status=live}}</ref> One of his artbooks also includes, among Rei's designs, a drawing of a dark-haired character named Yui Ichijō, although Sadamoto did not specify her identity.{{sfn|Sadamoto|2001|pp=72, 116}} Sadamoto conceived Asuka as occupying the position of an [[Japanese idol|idol]] in the world of ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' and as representing heterosexual desire; conversely, he conceived Rei as a symbol of motherhood,<ref>{{Cite magazine|date=December 1997|script-title=ja:貞本義行インタビュー|trans-title=Yoshiyuki Sadamoto Interview|magazine=[[Newtype]]|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|language=ja}}</ref> and as Asuka's opposite.<ref>Interview with Sadamoto in {{cite book|title=Der Mond: The Art of Yoshiyuki Sadamoto – Deluxe Edition|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|year=1999|isbn=978-4-04-853031-6|language=ja}}</ref> Anno also suggested giving Rei red eyes, which he believed would strengthen her personality and distinguish her from the other characters.<ref name=sadamoto/> Sadamoto changed her hair color to blue, echoing the protagonist of [[Aoki Uru|{{Transliteration|ja|Aoki Uru}}]], the unmade film sequel to ''[[Royal Space Force: The Wings of Honnêamise]]'' (1987).{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=164–165}} Sadamoto also gave her black stockings, inspired by a women's handball team he saw playing in middle school.<ref>Yoshiyuki Sadamoto's comment in {{Cite book|last=Hayashibara|first=Megumi|author-link=Megumi Hayashibara|script-title=ja:明日があるさ〜Sweet Time Express〜 ちょトク文庫版|date=2002|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|isbn=978-4-04-444502-7|language=ja|trans-title=There Is Always Tomorrow ~ Sweet Time Express ~ A Little Good Value Bunko Edition}}</ref> Black also ran counter to fashion at the time he designed Rei.<ref>{{Cite interview|last=Sakurai|first=Takamasa|subject-link=Takamasa Sakurai|title=Interview with Sadamoto Yoshiyuki|last2=Sadamoto|first2=Yoshiyuki|subject2-link=Yoshiyuki Sadamoto|last3=Kitamura|first3=Shizuka|script-website=ja:世界セイフク計画|trans-website=World Seifuku Project|url=http://sekai-seifuku.org/en/interview2_3/|access-date=March 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120304151905/http://sekai-seifuku.org/en/interview2_3/|archive-date=March 4, 2012|url-status=usurped|language=ja}}</ref>

===Development===
{{multiple image
|footer=Hideki Anno (left) incorporated concepts from Sigmund Freud (right) when he developed Rei.
|image1=Godzilla Resurgence World Premiere Red Carpet- Anno Hideaki (28526527191).jpg
|align=right
