}}

Anno drew on [[Sigmund Freud]]'s [[psychoanalysis|psychoanalytic]] concept of the [[Oedipus complex]] when developing Rei's role. He conceived the Evangelion and Rei as surrogate maternal figures for [[Shinji Ikari]]; Rei's emotional closeness to Shinji's father, [[Gendo Ikari]], added another layer to this Oedipal structure.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|p=86}} Sadamoto developed the erotic dimension of this dynamic in a more enigmatic and subdued form, contrasting with Anno's preference for more direct sexual expression.<ref>{{cite web|language=ja|access-date=April 29, 2020|first=Kazuya|last=Tsurumaki|author-link=Kazuya Tsurumaki|url=http://kodansha.cplaza.ne.jp/gainax/tsurumaki/tsurumaki_1st/tsurumaki_1st_scene3.html|script-title=ja:Scene3: 物語は絵に付随する|trans-title=Scene 3: The Story Is Attached to the Picture|script-website=ja:講談社 cplaza|trans-website=Kodansha C Plaza|publisher=[[Kodansha]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040605130346/http://kodansha.cplaza.ne.jp/gainax/tsurumaki/tsurumaki_1st/tsurumaki_1st_scene3.html|archive-date=June 5, 2004|url-status=dead}}</ref> Animator [[Mitsuo Iso]] proposed further ideas for the character. In his plans, Rei had a connection to {{nihongo foot|Arqa,|アルカ|Aruka|group=lower-alpha}}{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=159}} an ancient ruin located deep beneath Nerv headquarters that served as her metaphorical mother. Iso envisioned Rei dying upon experiencing her first menstruation.{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=139}} Rei fed on Arqa's flesh in another scenario inspired by [[Daijirō Morohoshi]]'s {{nihongo foot|''Kōshi Ankokuden'';|孔子暗黒伝||{{lit|''Dark Biography of Confucius''}}|group=lower-alpha}}{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=138}} Iso also associated her with a substance called {{nihongo foot|Adamah|アダマ|Adama|group=lower-alpha}} and proposed that only Gendo could touch her.{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=174}} He gave Rei the baptismal name {{nihongo foot|Karuya.|カルヤ|Karuya|group=lower-alpha}}{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=163}} Other proposals connected her existence to Gendo's attempts to recreate Yui and to a covenant with twelve final [[Angel (Neon Genesis Evangelion)|Angels]], which Iso envisioned with a mood reminiscent of [[Osamu Tezuka]]'s ''[[Dororo]]''.{{sfn|Iso|2023|p=179}}

During ''Evangelion''{{'}}s production and first broadcast, Anno struggled to write the character because he lacked personal interest in her and found her difficult to understand or relate to. He nevertheless regarded Rei as a representation of his [[unconscious mind]],{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=93–96}}<ref>{{Cite magazine|date=November 1996|script-title=ja:庵野秀明×上野峻哉の対談|trans-title=Hideaki Anno and Shunya Ueno's Interview|magazine=[[Newtype]]|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]}}</ref> and originally conceived her as "the unconscious Shinji".{{efn|Hideaki Anno described Rei in the original Japanese as, "{{lang|ja|無意識のシンジ君ってのは綾波レイ}}" ["The unconscious Shinji is Rei Ayanami"].<ref name=Waseda>{{Cite interview|author1=Jinken|last2=Anno|first2=Hideaki|subject2-link=Hideaki Anno|script-title=ja:庵野秀明 会見 2000.12.5|url=http://www.jinnken.org/kouenroku/annohideaki.html|access-date=February 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100223160931/http://www.jinnken.org/kouenroku/annohideaki.html|archive-date=February 23, 2010|url-status=dead|date=December 5, 2000|language=ja|trans-title=Interview with Hideaki Anno December 5, 2000}}</ref>}} Even in the fifth and sixth episodes, "[[Rei I]]" and "[[Rei II]]", which center on her character, Rei speaks only sparingly.<ref>{{cite book|last=Cannarsi|first=Gualtiero|title=Evangelion Encyclopedia|volume=3|pages=32–33|location=Granarolo dell'Emilia|publisher=[[Dynamic Italia]]|language=it}}</ref> Anno's lack of affinity with Rei led him to neglect her personality for much of the series and give her limited narrative space. Rei appears in only one scene in the seventh episode, "[[A Human Work]]", after Anno remembered to add her. Conversely, she never appears in the eighth installment, "[[Asuka Strikes!]]"{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=93–96}} As with other ''Evangelion'' characters, he incorporated aspects of his own life into Rei, including his vegetarianism and avoidance of meat.<ref>{{cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン・フィルムブック|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Film Book|volume=4|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|page=59|year=1996|isbn=978-4-04-852611-1|doi=10.11501/14567779}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last=Cannarsi|first=Gualtiero|title=Evangelion Encyclopedia|volume=6|pages=32, 41|location=Granarolo dell'Emilia|publisher=[[Dynamic Italia]]|language=it}}</ref> Early in production, Anno said that uncertainty about his company's future left him unsure what would happen to Rei and the other characters.<ref>{{harvnb|Sadamoto|1998|pp=170–171}}. "What were we trying to make here?", an essay by [[Hideaki Anno]].</ref> The character's backstory also changed during development. According to Sadamoto and assistant director [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], the staff originally conceived her as an alien entity before making her at least genetically human. In an interview, the two described Rei as possessing [[Yui Ikari]]'s human genes, and {{sic|those of the first Angel, [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Adam|Adam]]}}; Tsurumaki compared her condition to that of a Devilman, the human-demon hybrid from [[Go Nagai]]'s manga ''[[Devilman]]''.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=179–180}}

During production of the fourteenth episode, "[[Weaving a Story]]", Anno decided to explore Rei and her emotions through a monologue. When he was writing it, he sought to take her character in a [[schizophrenia|schizophrenic]] direction and considered how to portray madness. Someone lent him an issue of {{nihongo foot|{{Transliteration|ja|Bessatsu Takarajima}}|別冊宝島|group=lower-alpha}} about mental illness that included a poem by a person with a mental disorder; the poem stimulated his imagination and helped him write the monologue.<ref>{{cite web|last=Eng|first=Lawrence|url=http://www.cjas.org/~echen/articles/spring97/05_03b.html|title=In the Eyes of Hideaki Anno, Writer and Director of Evangelion|website=CJAS Newsletter Archives|access-date=June 15, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090709031758/http://www.cjas.org/~echen/articles/spring97/05_03b.html|archive-date=July 9, 2009|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite book|script-title=ja:残酷な天使のように|location=Tokyo|publisher=Magazine Magazine|year=1997|isbn=978-4-906011-25-4|trans-title=Like a Cruel Angel|chapter=庵野 秀明 – Part II|trans-chapter=Hideaki Anno – Part II|language=ja}}</ref> In the original script for the [[He was aware that he was still a child|twenty-first episode]], the first Rei clone survives [[Naoko Akagi]]'s strangulation; after briefly losing consciousness, she awakens alone in an empty command room.<ref>{{cite book|title=Evangelion Original III|isbn=978-4-8291-7323-7|date=1996|script-chapter=ja:ネルフ、誕生|trans-chapter=Nerv, Birth|pages=21-33|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Fujimi Shobo]]|doi=10.11501/14084471|language=ja}}</ref> Instead, the released episode shows Naoko killing this Rei and then herself.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=164}}{{sfn|Tokumu Kikan Chōsa Project Team|2007|p=246}} In "[[Rei III]]", one of the series' final episodes, the second Rei dies and a new clone replaces her, behaving similarly to a stranger toward Shinji.<ref name=Children/> Anno planned the second clone's death from the beginning.{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=97–100}} During production, Ikuhara, frustrated by fans' idealization and fetishization of Rei, suggested subverting their expectations by portraying her as an ordinary girl who marries and becomes pregnant by the end of the series, but Anno rejected Ikuhara's idea.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Interview with Hideaki Anno|magazine=Monthly Anime Style|date=April 2000|language=ja|publisher=Style}}</ref> Anno's original plans for the finale did not include the giant Rei who later appeared in ''[[The End of Evangelion]]'' (1997); he conceived the idea afterward.{{efn|Hideaki Anno: "{{lang|ja|もともとテレビの26っていうのはああなる予定だったんです。[...] 綾波レイが巨大化するっていうのは当初の予定に無いですけど、まあどうせやるなら地球規模にしようと。}}" ["Originally, I had planned that the television version of episode 26 would end up being like [it was]. [...] I didn't expect at the time that Ayanami would become enormous, though. I felt, well, if we're going to do it let's make her the size of the planet."]<ref name=Waseda/>}} Anno considered Rei's character arc essentially complete after her smile in the sixth episode because she and Shinji had connected with each other in that scene.{{sfn|Anno|1997b|p=96}} He compared Rei's development after the scene in which she smiles at Shinji, followed by a regression in interpersonal communication, to that of Hideki Gō from ''[[Return of Ultraman]]''; Gō grows closer to the other members of the Monster Attack Team, only to become estranged from them soon afterward. Anno attributed this pattern to his own mistrust and fear of communicating with others.{{efn|Hideaki Anno: "{{lang|ja|僕の他人に対するコミュニケーションの不信感とか、恐怖っていうのが出ていますね、その辺は。}}" ["For instance, in my distrust of communications with other people, a feeling of fear comes out of that sort."]<ref>{{harvnb|Anno|1997b|pp=93–97}}</ref>}}{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=93–97}}

===Voice actresses===
[[Megumi Hayashibara]] voiced Rei in the original series and all of the character's subsequent appearances, including the later films, spin-offs, video games, and the ''[[Rebuild of Evangelion]]'' film series. In 1995, Hayashibara said Rei's laconic personality intrigued her and presented a new challenge.<ref>{{Cite interview|last=Hayashibara|first=Megumi|subject-link=Megumi Hayashibara|last2=Mitsuishi|first2=Kotono|subject-link2=Kotono Mitsuishi|url=http://www.usagi.org/doi/seiyuu/radio/boogie/19950325.html|access-date=April 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121113131332/http://www.usagi.org/doi/seiyuu/radio/boogie/19950325.html|archive-date=November 13, 2012|website=Seiyuu Database|script-title=ja:林原めぐみの東京ブギーナイト – 1995.03.25|trans-title=Megumi Hayashibara's Tokyo Boogie Night – 1995.03.25|url-status=live}}</ref> Hayashibara also auditioned for Asuka and [[Misato Katsuragi]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Yalcinkaya|first=Günseli|access-date=May 3, 2021|url=https://www.dazeddigital.com/film-tv/article/52614/1/neon-genesis-evangelion-at-25-an-oral-history-of-the-legendary-anime|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' at 25: An Oral History of the Legendary Anime|website=[[Dazed]]|date=April 30, 2021|archive-date=May 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210503150808/https://www.dazeddigital.com/film-tv/article/52614/1/neon-genesis-evangelion-at-25-an-oral-history-of-the-legendary-anime|url-status=live}}</ref> Anno cast her as Rei after hearing her performance in the [[original video animation]] {{nihongo foot|''Ichigatsu ni wa Christmas'',|一月にはChristmas|group=lower-alpha}} believing her voice better suited the character.<ref>{{cite AV media|language=ja|script-title=ja:林原めぐみのHeartful Station|trans-title=Megumi Hayashibara's Heartful Station|date=May 16, 2004}}</ref> Hayashibara recalled that few taciturn, emotionally cold characters existed in anime before ''Neon Genesis Evangelion''; lacking models to imitate, she developed her own approach to Rei.<ref name=Fukuda>{{cite web|last=Fukuda|first=Makoto|url=http://www.yomiuri.co.jp/dy/features/arts/T110629002802.htm|title=Choice Voice / Megumi Hayashibara Now Part of Anime History|website=Daily Yomiuri Online|publisher=[[Yomiuri Shimbun]]|date=July 1, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110707071459/http://www.yomiuri.co.jp/dy/features/arts/T110629002802.htm|archive-date=July 7, 2011|access-date=April 20, 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref> During the recording sessions, Hideaki Anno guided Hayashibara and instructed her to deliver her lines in the flattest tone possible. She recalled his explanation: "It's not that she doesn't have emotion, but that she doesn't know what it is".<ref name=Voice>{{harvnb|Sadamoto|2012|pp=516–517}}. "The Voices in Your Head That You Pass Without Hearing", an essay by [[Megumi Hayashibara]].</ref> According to Hayashibara, Rei's inability to understand emotion leaves no distinction between her words and feelings.<ref name=Voice/> She also attributed Rei's appeal to her lack of calculated pretense, arguing that her beauty stems from the fact that "she has feelings", and said that discovering the warmth hidden beneath Rei's detached exterior allowed her to relate to the character.<ref name=Voice/>

