When Hayashibara was voicing the [[mahjong]] game {{nihongo foot|{{Transliteration|ja|Shinseiki Evangelion: Eva to Yukai na Nakamatachi}},|新世紀エヴァンゲリオンエヴァと愉快な仲間たち||{{lit|Neon Genesis Evangelion: Eva and Good Friends}}|group=lower-alpha}} she said she came to understand the difference between "lack of intonation" and "absence of emotion" in speech. She interpreted a lack of intonation as a sign of "self-confidence"; in her view, insecure or insincere people tend to emphasize their words, whereas Rei speaks plainly because she is honest and truthful.{{efn|Megumi Hayashibara: "{{lang|ja|最近わかったことなんですけど、言葉に抑揚がないっていうのは、感情がないっていうのではなくて、ひとつの自信の現れでもあるのかなって思うようになったんですよ。}}" ["Lately, I understood it, but as for the lack of intonation in the words, and the absence of emotion, I thought like there was an expression of self-confidence."]<ref>{{Cite web|script-title=ja:綾波レイ|trans-title=Rei Ayanami|url=http://www.gainax.co.jp/soft/mahjong/ss/chara/rei.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20011227153221/http://www.gainax.co.jp/soft/mahjong/ss/chara/rei.html|archive-date=December 27, 2001|access-date=April 20, 2020|language=ja|website=[[Gainax]]|url-status=deviated}}</ref>}} Hayashibara reprised the role for the ''Rebuild'' series. During production of the third film, ''[[Evangelion: 3.0 You Can (Not) Redo]]'' (2012), she recorded scenes before the animation was complete and therefore asked Anno to explain the action to her. She had to voice Rei as indifferent and take care to convey her feelings of happiness with the right nuance.<ref name="Qbook">{{Cite book|script-title=ja:ヱヴァンゲリヲン新劇場版:Q 記録集|date=November 17, 2012|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Khara (studio)|Khara]]|pages=50–52|language=ja|trans-title=Evangelion New Theatrical Edition: Q: Record Edition|oclc=1423284791}}</ref>

Regarding the final installment, ''[[Evangelion: 3.0+1.0 Thrice Upon a Time]]'' (2021), Hayashibara found it difficult to portray another Rei clone as she gradually matures; the staff criticized her both when she tried to make Rei sound pure and when she gave her a less emotional performance. Anno wanted the characters to sound younger and express fuller emotions, making it difficult for Hayashibara to capture the nuances he sought.{{sfn|Hikawa|Kimata|2021|pp=22–24}} Rei's role helped establish her as an icon of anime fandom.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://animeacademy.com/profile_hayashibara_megumi.php|title=Profile: Hayashibara Megumi|access-date=April 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101119132336/http://animeacademy.com/profile_hayashibara_megumi.php|archive-date=November 19, 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Mainichi">{{Cite web|author=Mantanweb|date=August 26, 2007|script-title=ja:特集: ヱヴァ、新生 新劇場版、庵野は何を目指すのか? |trans-title=Special: ''Eva'', Newly Created New Theatrical Edition, What Is Anno Aiming For?|url=http://mainichi.jp/enta/mantan/archive/news/2007/08/26/20070826org00m200002000c.html|script-website=ja:毎日jp|trans-website=Mainichi.jp|publisher=[[The Mainichi Newspapers Co.]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090707092937/http://mainichi.jp/enta/mantan/archive/news/2007/08/26/20070826org00m200002000c.html|archive-date=July 7, 2009|access-date=March 3, 2014|language=ja|url-status=dead}}</ref> Shunsuke Nozawa, an anthropologist, noted an explosion of interest in Japanese voice actors during the 1990s and placed Hayashibara at the center of this shift, attributing it to the "enormous, societal-level fascination" with Ayanami.<ref name="Nozawa">{{cite web|last=Nozawa|first=Shunsuke|url=https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/effacement/|title=Effacement|website=Japanese Media and Popular Culture|publisher=[[University of Tokyo]]|date=April 21, 2020|access-date=November 2, 2021|archive-date=October 7, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211007175916/https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/effacement/|url-status=live}}</ref> As a result, Hayashibara received interviews about the series and regular invitations to appear on television as a celebrity.<ref name="Nozawa"/>

[[Amanda Winn-Lee]] voices Rei in English in the original series and the Amazon dubs of the ''Rebuild of Evangelion'' films.<ref>Sources for Amanda Winn-Lee voicing the character in English:
* {{harvnb|Espejo|2025|p=123}}
* {{Cite web|last=Hodgkins|first=Crystalyn|date=August 7, 2021|title=Amazon Prime Video Lists New English Dub Cast for 1st 3 ''Rebuild of Evangelion'' Films|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2021-08-07/amazon-prime-video-lists-new-english-dub-cast-for-1st-3-rebuild-of-evangelion-films/.175998|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210807161927/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2021-08-07/amazon-prime-video-lists-new-english-dub-cast-for-1st-3-rebuild-of-evangelion-films/.175998|archive-date=August 7, 2021|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Anime News Network]]}}
* {{Cite web|last=Liu|first=Stephanie|date=2021|title=New English Dub Announced for Amazon Prime ''Evangelion'' Films|url=https://www.siliconera.com/new-english-dub-announced-for-amazon-prime-evangelion-films/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520171003/https://www.siliconera.com/new-english-dub-announced-for-amazon-prime-evangelion-films/|archive-date=May 20, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Siliconera]]}}</ref> According to Winn-Lee, Rei retains a sense of humanity beneath her cold, detached exterior despite her low self-esteem and awareness that others consider her expendable.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Lee|first=Roderick|title=Meet the Voice of AD Vision: Amanda Winn|url=http://www.ex.org/2.5/12-news_winn.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060110085818/http://www.ex.org/2.5/12-news_winn.html|archive-date=January 10, 2006|access-date=April 25, 2020|magazine=EX: The Online World of Anime and Manga|volume=2|issue=5|url-status=dead}}</ref> Winn-Lee also said that Rei "knows she's expendable", but stressed that "she's still human", which prevented her from portraying the character as completely catatonic.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fansview.com/080798h.htm|title=Otakon Highlights – ''Evangelion'' Voice Actors|date=August 7, 1998|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080617215942/http://www.fansview.com/080798h.htm|archive-date=June 17, 2008|access-date=April 25, 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Brina Palencia]] voices Rei in Funimation's dub of the ''Rebuild of Evangelion'' films,<ref name="Liu">{{Cite web|last=Liu|first=Stephanie|date=2021|title=New English Dub Announced for Amazon Prime ''Evangelion'' Films|url=https://www.siliconera.com/new-english-dub-announced-for-amazon-prime-evangelion-films/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520171003/https://www.siliconera.com/new-english-dub-announced-for-amazon-prime-evangelion-films/|archive-date=May 20, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026|website=[[Siliconera]]}}</ref> and Ryan Bartley voices her in the [[Netflix]] dub.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Maki|first=Vanessa|date=June 18, 2022|title=3 of the Best Female Characters in {{'}}''Neon Genesis Evangelion''{{'}}|url=https://www.themarysue.com/best-female-characters-in-neon-genesis-evangelion/|access-date=June 21, 2026|website=The Mary Sue|archive-date=December 16, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251216003621/https://www.themarysue.com/best-female-characters-in-neon-genesis-evangelion/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Appearances==
===''Neon Genesis Evangelion''===
The series introduces Rei Ayanami as an enigmatic girl who pilots an Evangelion, a giant biomechanical humanoid deployed by special agency Nerv against hostile beings called Angels. Rei attends school with Shinji Ikari,{{sfn|Bryce|Cheung|Gutierrez|2010|p=286}} the series's protagonist and a fellow Evangelion pilot, and is later revealed to be a clone of his mother, Yui Ikari.<ref>Sources used for demonstrating Rei is a clone:
* {{harvnb|Napier|2002|pp=424–426}}
* {{harvnb|Sellés de Lucas|Hernández-Pérez|2024|pp=226–227}}
* {{harvnb|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=124}}</ref> She is considered the ''First Child'',<ref name="Character Interrelations">{{harvnb|Lameiro-González|2021|p=358}}</ref> or ''First Children'' in the Japanese version.{{sfn|Muir|2023|p=43}} In 2014, she enrolls at Tokyo-3's First Municipal Middle School.{{sfn|Giorgi|Morando|Poggio|2008|p=77}} In 2015, the Angels begin attacking the city, prompting Nerv's Commander Gendo Ikari to summon his son, Shinji, to pilot an Eva unit.{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=124}} When Shinji refuses to pilot, Rei first appears severely injured and physically frail. Seeing Rei's condition and feeling sympathy for her, Shinji ultimately agrees to pilot the Evangelion in her place.{{sfn|Smith|2023}}{{sfn|Tsukamoto|2022|pp=37–38}} In the episode "[[Don't Be]]", the Angel [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Arael|Arael]] subjects Rei's fellow pilot Asuka Langley Soryu to a psychic attack that forces her to relive her painful past. Gendo then orders Rei to retrieve a weapon named Lance of Longinus from underground and use it against the Angel. Rei retrieves the lance and hurls it at her target.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|pp=165–166}} As the series progresses, Rei pilots her assigned Evangelion unit under Gendo's command and develops a friendship with Shinji, gradually changing her attitude toward others.<ref name="Character Interrelations"/>

In the twenty-third episode, "[[Rei III]]", Rei decides to sacrifice herself alongside Evangelion Unit-00 in the battle with the Angel [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Armisael|Armisael]] to save Shinji and destroy the enemy. After her apparent death, Dr. [[Ritsuko Akagi]] reveals that Rei was born at the Laboratory for Artificial Evolution's third branch, located beneath [[Lake Ashino]] in [[Hakone]].{{sfn|Porori|2010|p=77}} Rei's blue-haired body derives from Yui Ikari,{{sfn|Bryce|Cheung|Gutierrez|2010|p=285}} who lost her life during an experiment with Evangelion Unit-01.<ref name=Miller>{{harvnb|Miller|2012|p=86}}</ref> Ritsuko also reveals that Nerv keeps numerous Rei clones in the deepest level of its headquarters, allowing another clone to replace each Rei after her death.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|pp=167–168}}{{sfn|Torrents González|Ballús Santacana|2023|p=154}} Rei also incorporates the soul of [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Lilith|Lilith]], the second Angel.<ref>{{cite journal|editor=Gainax|title=用語集|journal=The End of Evangelion Program Book|year=1997|language=ja}}</ref> Nerv stores her memories in a spinal columnar object inside the Dummy Plug Plant in Central Dogma.{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=176–177}} A flashback in the twenty-first episode, "[[He was aware that he was still a child]]", reveals that in 2010, Gendo brought the first Rei clone, Rei I, to the Gehirn base, the predecessor of Nerv that developed and constructed the first Evangelion units, introducing her as the daughter an acquaintance had entrusted to him.<ref>{{cite book|editor=[[Gainax]]|page=40|title=Death & Rebirth Program Book|date=1997|language=ja|edition=Special}}</ref> Dr. Naoko Akagi, a romantic rival to Yui{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=164}} and a colleague of Gendo, killed this first Rei.{{sfn|Murase|2000}}{{sfn|Thouny|2009|pp=129}} During her visit to Gehirn, Rei I became lost in the laboratory's control room and encountered Naoko, whom she called an "old hag" as if to provoke her.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=164}} She then revealed that Gendo used the same insult for Naoko. Recognizing Yui's features in the girl's face, Naoko flew into a rage and strangled Rei I before taking her own life.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=164}}<ref>{{cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン・フィルムブック|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Film Book|volume=8|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|pages=35–36|year=1996|isbn=978-4-04-852615-9|doi=10.11501/14591904}}</ref>

After her sacrifice, a third and final clone replaces Rei II. Ritsuko later destroys all of Rei's other bodies.<ref>{{cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン・フィルムブック|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Film Book|volume=9|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|pages=36–38|year=1997|isbn=978-4-04-852722-4|doi=10.11501/14588063}}</ref> Although this Rei clone shows greater emotional detachment,<ref name="Super/Heroes">{{harvnb|Malone|2007|p=115}}</ref> her bond with Shinji ultimately leads her to defy Gendo.<ref name="Muir">{{harvnb|Muir|2024|p=80}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last=Torrents|first=Alba G.|journal=Debats: Revista de cultura, poder i societat|issue=119|date=2013|language=es|title=La relación con lo materno en el cuerpo robótico en Neon Genesis Evangelion|publisher=Institució Alfons el Magnànim|p=66}}</ref> In the opening scenes of the film ''The End of Evangelion'' (1997), Rei III sees the disintegrated bodies of the other Ayanami clones floating in a tank.{{sfn|Afanasov|2020|p=60}} She later triggers the apocalyptic Third Impact and merges with Lilith, allowing Shinji to determine the course of Human Instrumentality, a process that merges humanity into a single collective consciousness.{{sfn|Porori|2010|pp=10–11, 100}} During Instrumentality, Rei assumes a gigantic form,<ref name="Super/Heroes"/>{{sfn|Seager|2023|p=237}} gathers humanity's souls,{{sfn|Seager|2023|p=234}} and develops a third eye. Shinji rejects Instrumentality, causing Rei's giant form to decompose. Afterward, as Shinji rematerializes, he briefly sees Rei watching him from a distance before she disappears.<ref>{{cite video|people=[[Hideaki Anno]], [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], [[Masayuki (animator)|Masayuki]] (directors)|title=Neon Genesis Evangelion: The End of Evangelion|title-link=Neon Genesis Evangelion: The End of Evangelion|medium=Film|publisher=[[Studio Gainax]]|date=1997|language=ja}}</ref>

===''Rebuild of Evangelion''===
Rei returns as a central character in ''Rebuild of Evangelion'', first appearing in the series's first installment, ''[[Evangelion: 1.0 You Are (Not) Alone]]'' (2007). She pilots Evangelion Unit-00 and helps Shinji defeat the Angel [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Ramiel|Ramiel]].<ref>{{cite video|people=[[Hideaki Anno]], [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], [[Masayuki (animator)|Masayuki]] (directors)|script-title=ja:ヱヴァンゲリヲン新劇場版: 序|trans-title=[[Evangelion: 1.0 You Are (Not) Alone]]|medium=Film|publisher=[[Studio Khara]]|date=2007|language=ja}}</ref> The ''Rebuild'' series places Shinji and Rei's relationship at the center of the story and expands their interactions compared with the original series.{{sfn|Lamarre|2010|p=351}} In the second film, ''[[Evangelion: 2.0 You Can (Not) Advance]]'' (2009), Rei develops a closer and more explicit relationship with Shinji than in the original series. In a departure from her original characterization, she attempts to host a dinner party for her fellow pilots.<ref>{{cite web|last=Theron|first=Martin|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/review/evangelion-2.22/you-can-not-advance/bluray|title=''Evangelion 2.22: You Can (Not) Advance''|date=March 31, 2011|website=[[Anime News Network]]|access-date=September 12, 2020|archive-date=June 15, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180615175808/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/review/evangelion-2.22/you-can-not-advance/bluray|url-status=live}}</ref> Anno originally considered this idea for the fourth episode of the original anime, "[[Hedgehog's Dilemma (Neon Genesis Evangelion)|Hedgehog's Dilemma]]", but abandoned it during production.<ref>{{harvnb|Khara|2010}}, 庵野 秀明 (Hideaki Anno) interview.</ref> At the climax, the Angel [[List of Angels in Neon Genesis Evangelion#Zeruel|Zeruel]] devours Rei and Unit-00. After Unit-01 goes out of control, Shinji enters the Angel and pulls Rei free, and the two embrace;{{sfn|Trap|2010a|pp=3–4}} the battle ends with both trapped inside Unit-01 and their actions triggering the Third Impact.<ref>{{cite video|people=[[Hideaki Anno]], [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], [[Masayuki (animator)|Masayuki]] (directors)|script-title=ja:ヱヴァンゲリヲン新劇場版: 破|trans-title=[[Evangelion: 2.0 You Can (Not) Advance]]|medium=Film|publisher=[[Studio Khara]]|date=2009|language=ja}}</ref> Fuyutsuki remarks that Shinji's behavior has improved; Gendo states that his plan requires Shinji and Rei to come together.{{sfn|Malone|2010|p=346}} After the film's release, Tsurumaki addressed the apparent plan to bring Shinji and Rei together, attributing that impression to the complexities of the script and expressing doubt that Anno had developed such a strategy in detail.<ref>{{harvnb|Khara|2010}}, 鶴巻 和哉 (Kazuya Tsurumaki) interview.</ref>

In the third installment, ''Evangelion: 3.0 You Can (Not) Redo'' (2012), set fourteen years later, another Rei clone takes the place of her predecessor and displays a colder, more taciturn personality.<ref>{{cite video|people=[[Hideaki Anno]], [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], [[Masayuki (animator)|Masayuki]] (directors)|script-title=ja:ヱヴァンゲリヲン新劇場版: Q|trans-title=[[Evangelion: 3.0 You Can (Not) Redo]]|medium=Film|publisher=[[Studio Khara]]|date=2012|language=ja}}</ref> In ''Evangelion: 3.0+1.0 Thrice Upon a Time'' (2021), the series' final film, the new clone, named Ayanami (Tentative Name), travels with Shinji and Asuka Shikinami to Village 3, a settlement whose inhabitants survived the Impacts and live isolated from the largely uninhabitable outside world. Here, Rei interacts with [[Toji Suzuhara]] and [[Hikari Horaki]], her former classmates who now live in the village as adults, as well as the village's women and children, and helps them work in the fields.<ref name="3.0+1.0">{{cite video|people=[[Hideaki Anno]], [[Kazuya Tsurumaki]], Katsuichi Nakayama, [[Mahiro Maeda]] (directors)|script-title=ja:シン・エヴァンゲリオン劇場版|trans-title=[[Evangelion: 3.0+1.0 Thrice Upon a Time]]|medium=Film|publisher=[[Studio Khara]]|date=2021|language=ja}}</ref> Through these experiences, she develops a sense of individuality, discovers everyday life, and learns to socialize with the villagers.<ref name="Ebisu">{{harvnb|Bechler|Muramatsu|2024|pages=121–122}}</ref>{{sfn|Tsukamoto|2022|p=46}} Eventually, Rei (Tentative Name), whose designation is Ayanami-Type No.006, loses the ability to maintain her form because she requires continuous exposure to the Evangelion's LCL liquid, and she dies in front of Shinji. During Instrumentality, Shinji reunites with the Rei from fourteen years earlier, who has remained inside Evangelion Unit-01. Now sporting visibly longer hair, Rei speaks with Shinji, who chooses to create a world free of Evangelions and give it a new beginning, Neon Genesis. The two bid each other a final farewell, shaking hands and smiling.<ref name="3.0+1.0"/>

Critics have identified [[posthumanist]] themes in the ''Rebuild of Evangelion'' films and Rei's new characterization. According to Quanyu Liao, Rei symbolizes "technological replication" and, as a clone, can survive in inhospitable and polluted areas.<ref name="Liao">{{harvnb|Liao|2024}}</ref> Liao also argued that Gendo and Seele's exploitation of Rei's clones epitomizes [[transhumanism]] and [[capitalism]].<ref name="Liao"/> Erick Agung Yohanes, Nita Novianti, and Nia Nafisah noted that Rei's clones show no emotion on their faces, emphasizing their artificial nature.{{sfn|Yohanes|Novianti|Nafisah|2025|p=137}} Daichi Nakagawa regarded Rei as an example of how artificial life can typify development better than human life,{{sfn|Nakagawa|2022|p=291}} and scholars Antonin Bechler and Kenjirō Muramatsu described Rei as portrayed in ''Evangelion 3.0+1.0'' as the "quintessential simulacrum"{{efn|The journal article states, "{{lang|fr|C'est ainsi qu'à travers le contact avec la réalité physique (travail manuel aux champs, bains, repas) et avec autrui, le personnage de Rei, simulacre par excellence, réapprend l'humanité.}}" ["It is thus through the contact with the physical reality (through manually in fields, baths, and meals) and with others, the person of Rei, the quintessential simulacrum, relearns the humanity."]<ref name="Ebisu"/>}} who attempts to connect with other humans.<ref name="Ebisu"/>

===Other appearances===
In Yoshiyuki Sadamoto's [[Neon Genesis Evangelion (manga)|manga adaptation]], Rei first appears in the first volume's second chapter, {{nihongo foot|"Reunion".|再会|Saikai|group=lower-alpha}}{{sfn|Sadamoto|2012|pp=65–66}}{{sfn|Sadamoto|1995|pp=67–68, 176}} She initially regards herself as empty and useless, believing that she exists solely to pilot the Evangelion under Gendo's orders, but physical contact with Shinji begins to change her outlook. In a scene in the manga's fifth volume, Rei burns herself when she prepares tea with Shinji at her apartment, and their hands briefly touch.{{sfn|Sadamoto|1999||pp=56–58}} Rei uses the occasion to encourage Shinji to talk to his father and open up to him. In a later chapter, she recalls the moment and wonders whether she will ever touch Shinji's hands again.{{sfn|Sadamoto|2011|pp=90–91}} During her battle with the Angel Armisael, Rei becomes increasingly aware of her sadness and affection for Shinji,{{sfn|Fornaroli|2012|p=2}} and expresses a desire to become one with him.<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 7, 2020|script-title=ja:"新世紀エヴァンゲリオン劇場版 Death (True)2 / Air / まごころを、君に"の感想と考察|trans-title=Impressions and Thoughts About ''Neon Genesis Evangelion: Death and Rebirth / The End of Evangelion''|url=https://good-movies.net/evangelion1998/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200707191147/https://good-movies.net/evangelion1998/|archive-date=July 7, 2020|access-date=July 5, 2025|website=Good Movies|language=ja}}</ref> Sadamoto's manga also alters Rei's characterization, making her more empathetic and receptive to human contact than her animated counterpart. It gives Rei greater prominence than Asuka, who plays the more prominent role in the anime. Sadamoto placed particular emphasis on Rei's relationship with Shinji, using the symbolism of their touching hands and the theme of motherhood, for which he drew inspiration from [[Kazuo Umezu]]'s ''[[The Drifting Classroom]]''.<ref>{{cite interview|author=Shinji Kakaroth|last2=Sadamoto|first2=Yoshiyuki|subject2-link=Yoshiyuki Sadamoto|author3=Eleonora "CaskaLangley"|access-date=September 12, 2020|url=http://www.nanoda.com/mmf-intervista-a-yoshiyuki-sadamoto/|title=MMF: Intervista a Yoshiyuki Sadamoto|trans-title=MMF: Interview with Yoshiyuki Sadamoto|date=July 10, 2013|language=it|archive-date=October 24, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171024014316/http://www.nanoda.com/mmf-intervista-a-yoshiyuki-sadamoto/}}</ref><ref name="Cut">{{Cite magazine|date=December 2014|script-title=ja:貞本 義行|trans-title=Yoshiyuki Sadamoto|magazine=CUT|publisher=Rockin'On|pages=54–59|language=ja}}</ref> Sadamoto described Shinji and Rei's feelings for each other as mutual love.<ref name=Cut/>

The animated series' final episode presents an alternate reality that departs from the preceding storyline.<ref>{{Cite episode|last=Anno|first=Hideaki|author-link=Hideaki Anno|last2=Tsurumaki|first2=Kazuya|author-link2=Kazuya Tsurumaki|author3=[[Masayuki (animator)|Masayuki]]|title=Take care of yourself.|episode-link=List of Neon Genesis Evangelion episodes|series=Neon Genesis Evangelion|series-link=Neon Genesis Evangelion|station=[[TV Tokyo]]|date=March 27, 1996|number=26|language=ja}}</ref> In this scenario, Rei joins Asuka and Shinji's class as a new student and displays a cheerful, absent-minded, and irascible personality.<ref>Sources for Rei's presentation in the last episode of ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'':
* {{harvnb|Miller|2012|pp=87–88}}
* {{harvnb|Galbraith|2019|p=118}}
* {{harvnb|Azuma|2009|p=38}}</ref> Spin-offs such as ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion: Angelic Days]]'', which takes place in the same alternate reality, retain this more outgoing characterization.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gainax.co.jp/soft/koutetsu2/contents.html|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion Iron Maiden 2nd''|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140203021257/http://www.gainax.co.jp/soft/koutetsu2/contents.html|archive-date=February 3, 2014|access-date=September 12, 2020|language=ja|website=Gainax Net|publisher=[[Gainax]]|url-status=deviated}}</ref> The original net animation ''[[Petit Eva: Evangelion@School]]'', a parody of the original series, features three Ayanami sisters: one has a diligent and introverted personality,{{sfn|Trap|2010b|pp=21–22}} another displays an outgoing, athletic personality,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.evangelion.co.jp/petit_eva/|script-title=ja:ぷちえゔぁ|trans-title=''Petit Eva''|access-date=September 12, 2020|language=ja|archive-date=July 14, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130714015826/http://www.evangelion.co.jp/petit_eva/|url-status=live|website=Evangelion}}</ref> and the third is a four-year-old who loves stuffed toys.<ref>{{cite book|title=Newtype USA|title-link=Newtype USA|volume=6|issue=6|page=67|date=June 2007|location=Houston|publisher=[[A.D. Vision]]}}</ref> ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion: Anima]]'', set three years after ''The End of Evangelion'' in an alternate timeline, introduces multiple incarnations of Rei: Rei Troi, who pilots an Evangelion named Evangelion Unit-02 Type II Allegorica;{{sfn|Yamashita|2010|p=12}} Rei Quatre; Rei Cinq; and the seven-year-old Rei Six. Each pilots an Evangelion-0.0 unit.{{sfn|Yamashita|2010|pp=33–34}} In ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion: Campus Apocalypse]]'', Rei's relationship with Kaworu attracts Shinji's attention.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Pattillo|first=Lissa|date=September 15, 2010|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion: Campus Apocalypse'' GN 1|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/review/neon-genesis-evangelion/campus-apocalypse/gn-1|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260303180700/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/review/neon-genesis-evangelion/campus-apocalypse/gn-1|archive-date=March 3, 2026|access-date=June 15, 2026|website=[[Anime News Network]]}}</ref> She also appears in ''Neon Genesis Evangelion: Legend of the Piko Piko Middle School Students''.{{sfn|Kawata|2017}}

Rei also appears in media outside the ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion (franchise)|Evangelion]]'' franchise. She appears as a playable character in ''[[Super Robot Wars]]'',<ref>{{Cite web|last=Stenbuck|first=Kite|date=November 24, 2023|title=Asuka Langley Will Pilot Shin Getter 1 in ''Super Robot Wars DD'' Crossover|url=https://www.siliconera.com/asuka-langley-will-pilot-shin-getter-1-in-super-robot-wars-dd-crossover/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251114175904/https://www.siliconera.com/asuka-langley-will-pilot-shin-getter-1-in-super-robot-wars-dd-crossover/|archive-date=November 14, 2025|access-date=June 28, 2026|website=Siliconera}}</ref> ''[[MapleStory]]'',<ref>{{Cite web|last=Luster|first=Joseph|date=May 17, 2023|title=''Maple Story'' Kicks Off ''Evangelion'' Crossover Event on February 28 [Updated]|url=https://www.crunchyroll.com/news/latest/2018/2/15/maple-story-kicks-off-evangelion-crossover-event-on-february-28|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240309180924/https://www.crunchyroll.com/news/latest/2018/2/15/maple-story-kicks-off-evangelion-crossover-event-on-february-28|archive-date=March 9, 2024|access-date=June 12, 2026|website=[[Crunchyroll News]]|publisher=[[Crunchyroll]]}}</ref> and ''[[Sonic Racing: CrossWorlds]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mäki|first=Jonas|date=June 6, 2026|title=''Sonic Racing: Crossworlds'' is getting visits from ''Godzilla'' and ''Evangelion''|url=https://www.gamereactor.eu/sonic-racing-crossworlds-is-getting-visits-from-godzilla-and-evangelion-1730513/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261018010409/https://www.gamereactor.eu/sonic-racing-crossworlds-is-getting-visits-from-godzilla-and-evangelion-1730513/|archive-date=October 18, 2026|access-date=June 28, 2026|website=Gamereactor UK|publisher=[[Gamez Publishing]]}}</ref> ''[[Tales of Zestiria]]''<ref>{{cite web|last=Green|first=Scott|url=http://www.crunchyroll.com/anime-news/2015/11/11/evangelion-costume-set-for-tales-of-zestiria-offered-in-america-and-europe|title=''"Evangelion''" Costume Set for "''Tales of Zestiria''" Offered in America and Europe|date=November 12, 2015|access-date=June 7, 2018|archive-date=June 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612142205/http://www.crunchyroll.com/anime-news/2015/11/11/evangelion-costume-set-for-tales-of-zestiria-offered-in-america-and-europe|url-status=live|website=[[Crunchyroll News]]|publisher=[[Crunchyroll]]}}</ref> and ''[[The First Descendant]]'' also offer her as [[downloadable content]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Lada|first=Jenni|date=June 12, 2026|title=''Evangelion'' Characters Join ''The First Descendant'' in New Crossover|url=https://www.siliconera.com/evangelion-characters-join-the-first-descendant-in-new-crossover/|access-date=June 13, 2026|website=[[Siliconera]]|archive-date=June 15, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260615011345/https://www.siliconera.com/evangelion-characters-join-the-first-descendant-in-new-crossover/|url-status=live}}</ref> She is featured in an official ''[[Shinkansen Henkei Robo Shinkalion]]'' crossover episode.<ref>{{cite web|last=Loveridge|first=Lynzee|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2018-08-09/rei-asuka-vas-confirmed-angel-themed-villain-revealed-for-shinkalion-giant-eva-episode/.135295|title=Rei, Asuka VAs Confirmed, Angel-Themed Villain Revealed for ''Shinkalion''{{'}}s Giant Eva Episode|website=[[Anime News Network]]|date=August 9, 2018|access-date=April 19, 2020|archive-date=May 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200506225231/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2018-08-09/rei-asuka-vas-confirmed-angel-themed-villain-revealed-for-shinkalion-giant-eva-episode/.135295|url-status=live}}</ref> The simulation game ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion: Ayanami Raising Project]]'' places the player in charge of caring for Rei.<ref name="Struggle for Imagination">{{harvnb|Galbraith|2019|p=118}}</ref> Players can also pursue Rei as a romantic interest in ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion: Girlfriend of Steel 2nd]]''<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wong|first=Alistair|date=November 23, 2016|title=Fan Translation Explores Shinji's Relationships In This PSP ''Evangelion'' Visual Novel|url=https://www.siliconera.com/fan-translation-explores-shinjis-relationships-psp-evangelion-visual-novel/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251018201723/https://www.siliconera.com/fan-translation-explores-shinjis-relationships-psp-evangelion-visual-novel/|archive-date=October 18, 2025|access-date=June 19, 2026|website=[[Siliconera]]}}</ref> and ''[[Neon Genesis Evangelion 2]]''.<ref>{{Cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン2攻略ガイド|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion 2 Strategy Guide|date=2003|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|isbn=978-4-04-707137-7|location=Tokyo|language=ja}}</ref> In the manga adaptation of ''Neon Genesis Evangelion: Shinji Ikari Raising Project'', Rei becomes Shinji's distant cousin<ref>{{Cite web|last=Takahashi|first=Osamu|translator-last=Gombos|translator-first=Michael|date=July 8, 2009|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion: The Shinji Ikari Raising Project Volume 1''|url=https://www.darkhorse.com/Books/14-732/Neon-Genesis-Evangelion-The-Shinji-Ikari-Raising-Project-Volume-1-TPB|url-status=live|access-date=February 27, 2021|website=Dark Horse Comics|archive-date=July 5, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170705032702/http://www.darkhorse.com/Books/14-732/Neon-Genesis-Evangelion-The-Shinji-Ikari-Raising-Project-Volume-1-TPB}}</ref> and transfers to his school, complicating his relationship with Asuka.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Raiteri|first=Steve|date=May 15, 2010|title=Graphic Novels|magazine=[[Library Journal]]|pages=66|volume=135|issue=9|location=New York|publisher=[[Media Source Inc.]]}}</ref> ''Neon Genesis Evangelion RPG'', a [[tabletop role-playing game]] adaptation of ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'', includes Rei as a playable character.{{sfn|Nakazawa|1996|pp=27, 56}}

==Characterization and themes==
[[File:War damage on Sarajevo buildings (14024736919).jpg|thumb|upright=1.1|alt=Damage on buildings in Sarajevo|Cultural critic [[Hiroki Azuma]] associated Rei's living environment with images of refugees and trauma, discussing a comparison between Rei and post-war conditions including those in [[Bosnian War|Bosnia]].]]

Rei is an enigmatic, unhappy and taciturn girl with few social connections who readily follows orders. She is introverted, uncomplaining, socially detached, and aloof.<ref>Sources demonstrating Rei's characteristics:
* {{Cite web|last=Álvarez|first=Por Raúl|date=November 15, 2013|title=Top 20 mejores personajes de series anime|trans-title=Top 20 Best Characters of Anime Series|url=https://es.ign.com/movies/69949/top-20-mejores-personajes-de-series-anime?p=3|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261018010553/https://es.ign.com/movies/69949/top-20-mejores-personajes-de-series-anime?p=3|archive-date=October 18, 2026|access-date=September 25, 2026|website=IGN España|language=es-es}}
* {{harvnb|Martinez|2015|p=79}}
* {{harvnb|Makino|Fujimoto|Tasaki|Yamanishi|2025|p=3193}}
* {{harvnb|Ashcraft|Ueda|2010|pp=182–183}}
* {{harvnb|McCarthy|2008|p=71}}
* {{cite web|last=Crandol|first=Mike|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/feature/2002-06-11|title=Understanding ''Evangelion''|date=June 11, 2002|website=[[Anime News Network]]|access-date=April 25, 2020|archive-date=May 18, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190518114503/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/feature/2002-06-11|url-status=live}}
* {{cite magazine|editor-last=Graham|editor-first=Miyako|title=Anecdotes from Mr. Hideaki Anno|pages=40–41|magazine=Protoculture Addicts|issue=43|year=1996|location=Quebec|publisher=Protoculture}}
* {{harvnb|Thouny|2009|pp=129}}
* {{harvnb|Chen|Ji|2022|p=116}}</ref> Hideaki Anno described Rei as a very quiet, taciturn girl who rarely complains and said that she was created solely to pilot an Evangelion.<ref name="Protoculture Addicts">{{cite magazine|editor-last=Graham|editor-first=Miyako|title=Anecdotes from Mr. Hideaki Anno|pages=40–41|magazine=Protoculture Addicts|issue=43|year=1996|location=Quebec|publisher=Protoculture}}</ref> Sociologist Satomi Ishikawa analyzed a scene in the series' sixth episode, "Rei II", in which Shinji asks Rei why she pilots Evangelion Unit-00. Rei answers that piloting provides her only connection with other people, expressing her total commitment to fighting the Angels, even at the cost of her life.<ref>Sources providing reasoning for piloting Evangelion Unit-00:
* {{harvnb|Ishikawa|2007|p=75}}
* {{Cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン(ニュータイプ100%コレクション)|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|year=2005|page=34|language=ja|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Newtype 100% Collection|doi=10.11501/14577331|isbn=978-4-04-852700-2}}
* {{harvnb|Ashcraft|Ueda|2010|pp=182–183}}
</ref> Rei also says she wants to die and disappear from existence.<ref>Sources for Rei's statements about death:
* {{harvnb|Napier|2002|pp=426–427}}
* {{harvnb|Bryce|Cheung|Gutierrez|2010|p=288}}
* {{harvnb|Ashby|2008}}
</ref> Japanese critic [[Tamaki Saitō]] argued that Rei's existence has no solid basis, "she knows no trauma, and she lacks motivation".{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=160}} Chris Struckmann interpreted Rei and Shinji as counterparts to the traditional heroic figures of Japanese animation and noted that, despite Rei's apparent fragility, she possesses an "icy decisive mind".<ref name="Anime Impact">{{harvnb|Struckmann|2018}}</ref> During a show, a boy asked Anno what Rei likes, and Anno replied that he had never thought about it.<ref>{{cite web|last=Sevakis|first=Justin|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/buried-treasure/2007-05-03|title=Hideaki Anno Talks to Kids|website=[[Anime News Network]]|date=May 3, 2007|access-date=March 12, 2021|archive-date=July 29, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130729075339/http://www.animenewsnetwork.com/buried-treasure/2007-05-03|url-status=live}}</ref> According to him, she does not appreciate her own life, hurts herself, and feels she does not need friends, being aware of her replaceability: "Her presence, her existence—ostensible existence—is ephemeral. She's a very sad girl. She only has the barest minimum of what she needs to have."<ref name=Atlantic/> Anno also attributed the Japanese attraction to characters such as Rei to "a stunted imaginative landscape born of Japan's defeat in the Second World War" and remarked on the lack of preparation for adulthood in Japanese education after the Second World War.<ref name=Atlantic>{{cite web|last=Samuels|first=David|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2007/05/let-s-die-together/305776/|title=Let's Die Together|date=May 1, 2007|website=[[The Atlantic]]|publisher=[[The Atlantic Monthly Group]]|access-date=October 15, 2026|archive-date=March 31, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150331180852/http://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2007/05/let-s-die-together/305776/|url-status=live}}</ref>

For critic Gerald Alva Miller, Rei experiences alienation and existential angst beneath her cold demeanor.{{sfn|Miller|2012|p=85}} Critics have linked her silent and inexpressive personality to [[alexithymia]] or a [[schizoid personality disorder]].{{sfn|Clements|McCarthy|2006|p=185}}{{sfn|Mizobe|2011|p=48}} An official ''Death and Rebirth'' booklet compared her to a blank [[noh]] mask;<ref name=Children>{{cite magazine|title=Children|magazine=Death & Rebirth Program Book (Special Edition)|year=1997|publisher=[[Gainax]]|language=ja|url=http://www.evaotaku.com/html/dr2-children.html|access-date=April 25, 2020|archive-date=August 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200808201305/http://www.evaotaku.com/html/dr2-children.html|url-status=live}}</ref> the book ''Schizo / Neon Genesis Evangelion'' characterized her as "a brain that does not see dreams, completely separated from [[Carl Gustav Jung|Jung]]'s [[collective unconscious]]".{{efn|Hideaki Anno: "{{lang|ja|綾波レイとは何か? それは、ユングの集合的無意識から完全に見離された、夢を見ない脳。}}" ["What is Rei Ayanami? As for that, a brain that does not see dreams, completely separated from Jung's collective unconscious."]<ref>{{harvnb|Anno|1997a|p=189}}</ref>}} Gualtiero Cannarsi, who curated the Italian adaptation of the series, similarly described Rei as lacking basic life skills because no one taught them to her, leaving her uninterested in such matters. Her apartment reflects this attitude through its neglected hygiene. In one episode, Ritsuko Akagi remarks that Rei, like Commander Ikari, lacks practical life skills.<ref name="Gualtiero Cannarsi">{{cite book|last=Cannarsi|first=Gualtiero|title=Evangelion Encyclopedia|volume=3|page=27|location=Granarolo dell'Emilia|publisher=[[Dynamic Italia]]|language=it}}</ref> Its floor is littered with scientific texts on biological interactions and genetics written in the Latin alphabet in the original series<ref>{{cite book|last=Cannarsi|first=Gualtiero|title=Evangelion Encyclopedia|volume=5|page=18|location=Granarolo dell'Emilia|publisher=[[Dynamic Italia]]|language=it}}</ref> and ''[[The Happy Prince and Other Tales]]'' in the ''Rebuild of Evangelion'' series.<ref>{{Cite book|script-title=ja:ヱヴァンゲリヲン新劇場版:序 全記録全集ビジュアルストーリー版・設定 資料版|date=2008|location=Tokyo|publisher=Khara|page=321|language=ja|trans-title=Evangelion 1.0: You Are (Not) Alone – All Records Complete Collection Visual Story Edition ・ Set Data Edition|isbn=978-4-905033-18-9}}</ref> Hiroki Azuma argued that Anno combined images of refugees and trauma, which he associated with postwar crises such as the [[Bosnian War|conflict in Bosnia]], with the scientific, starkly undecorated appearance of Rei's room.<ref name=Azuma>{{cite web|last=Azuma|first=Hiroki|author-link=Hiroki Azuma|title=Animé or Something Like it: ''Neon Genesis Evangelion''|url=http://www.ntticc.or.jp/pub/ic_mag/ic018/intercity/higashi_E.html|publisher=[[NTT InterCommunication Center]]|access-date=August 13, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120808182031/http://www.ntticc.or.jp/pub/ic_mag/ic018/intercity/higashi_E.html|archive-date=August 8, 2012|url-status=live}}</ref> ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' character designer Yoshiyuki Sadamoto compared her disposition to a shadow and air, describing her as someone people desire but cannot attain.<ref name=sadamoto/> According to Sadamoto, Rei experiences emotions but struggles to express them or communicate with others.<ref>{{harvnb|Sadamoto|2012|p=515}}. Commentary to the third ''Eva'' volume.</ref> He also described Rei and Asuka as characters with different strengths.<ref>{{Cite interview|interviewer=[[Young Ace]]|title=Yoshiyuki Sadamoto ''Young Ace'' interview|url=http://theplanetsthatmatter.com/features/yoshiyuki_sadamoto_young_ace_interview/yoshiyuki_sadamoto_young_ace_interview.html|access-date=April 30, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091125160538/http://theplanetsthatmatter.com/features/yoshiyuki_sadamoto_young_ace_interview/yoshiyuki_sadamoto_young_ace_interview.html|archive-date=November 25, 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref> Hayashibara viewed Rei as celestial and said her fluctuating emotions required meticulous attention during her performance to avoid repeating takes.<ref name=Qbook/> Hoon J. Lee also observed that Rei rescues Asuka when she becomes overwhelmed by her suffering and loses the will to act.{{sfn|Lee|2025|p=112}}

From the series's opening episodes, Rei struggles to interpret Shinji's words and actions in his attempts to connect with her. Although the two pilots occasionally converse, they fail to communicate on an emotional level or understand each other's feelings.<ref>{{cite book|last=Cannarsi|first=Gualtiero|title=Evangelion Encyclopedia|volume=3|page=35|location=Granarolo dell'Emilia|publisher=[[Dynamic Italia]]|language=it}}</ref> After a major battle in the sixth episode, Shinji cries with relief at finding Rei alive, prompting her to smile,{{sfn|Trap|2010c|p=11}} a major turning point in her character development.<ref>{{cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン・フィルムブック|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Film Book|volume=2|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|page=66|year=1996|isbn=978-4-04-852609-8|doi=10.11501/14584385}}</ref> Their exchange brings them to a point of mutual understanding.<ref name=Children/> For critic Manabu Tsuribe, Rei's smile in the sixth episode marks the climax of ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' and, in terms of the "growth and independence of a boy", the conclusion of its [[Bildungsroman]]-like character arc.<ref>{{cite web|last=Tsuribe|first=Manabu|url=http://www001.upp.so-net.ne.jp/tsuribe/anime/critique/evae.html|title=Prison of Self-Consciousness: An Essay on ''Evangelion''|website=Landolt|date=February 1999|access-date=August 23, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170721105429/http://www001.upp.so-net.ne.jp/tsuribe/anime/critique/evae.html|archive-date=July 21, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> Scholar Emily Muir described her relationship with Shinji in the early episodes as protective. Later in the series, Rei begins to question her previous loyalty to Gendo, ultimately rebelling against him and rescuing Shinji by detonating her own Evangelion unit. Muir interpreted this development as the emergence of "[[human will]]".<ref name="Muir"/> In the twenty-third episode, she decides to save Shinji from an Angel attack;{{sfn|Brophy|2024|p=530}} Mio Bryce, Paul Cheung, and Anna Katrina Gutierrez interpreted the scene as a demonstration of her humanity.{{sfn|Bryce|Cheung|Gutierrez|2010|p=287}} Academic [[Frenchy Lunning]] described Rei as an [[Anima and animus|anima]] of the male proagonist.{{sfn|Lunning|2007|p=281}} Through her relationship with Shinji, Rei becomes increasingly aware of her own identity and desires.{{sfn|Porori|2010|p=86}}{{sfn|Porori|2010|p=7}} Critics have linked their relationship to Rei's background as a genetic clone of Shinji's mother,{{sfn|Rayhert|2018|p=167}} suggesting that their bond stems from and is strengthened by Rei's maternal nature.{{sfn|Lozano-Méndez|Loriguillo-López|2022|p=315}} Kazuya Tsurumaki described Shinji's feelings for Rei as sexual and incestuous; conversely, Kentaro Takekuma called her an "eternal virgin".{{efn|Kentaro Takekuma: "{{lang|ja|永遠の処女というかね。}}" ["Or rather, she is an eternal virgin, right?"]<ref>{{harvnb|Anno|1997a|pp=177–179}}</ref>}} According to screenwriter [[Yōji Enokido]], she produces in young men a feeling of distance as though they still depend on their mothers.<ref>{{harvnb|Khara|2010}}, 榎戸 洋司 (Yōji Enokido) interview.</ref> In the first episode, Shinji also sees a ghost of Rei in a deserted city near Tokyo-3. This apparition differs from the physical Rei and has been linked to the scenario of the film ''The End of Evangelion'', released in 1997 as a conclusion to the classic series, in which people undergoing Instrumentality see apparitions of Rei shortly before they die.{{sfn|Ballús|Torrents|2014|p=285}} [[Yūichirō Oguro]], an editor of some of the contents of the Japanese home video editions of ''Evangelion'', wrote that the ghost of Rei that Shinji sees on the avenue is "the existence that gazes upon man" and that the scene symbolizes his mother's protection throughout the series.<ref>{{cite book|title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Platinum Edition Booklet|volume=1|publisher=[[ADV Manga|ADV]]|date=2004}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Oguro|first=Yūichirō|date=May 18, 2006|script-title=ja:第34回 エヴァ雑記"第壱話 使徒、襲来"|trans-title=The 34th ''Eva'' Miscellaneous Notes "First Episode Angel Attack"|url=http://www.style.fm/as/05_column/animesama34.shtml|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191225100132/http://www.style.fm/as/05_column/animesama34.shtml|archive-date=December 25, 2019|access-date=April 17, 2020|script-website=ja:Webアニメスタイル|trans-website=Web Anime Style|publisher=Studio You|language=ja}}</ref>

