In an interview with Hideaki Anno, Japanese academic Mitsunari Ohizumi compared Rei to the young women who joined the Japanese religious group [[Aum Shinrikyō]], which carried out the [[Tokyo subway sarin attack]] in 1995, noting their complete dependence on the group's leader, [[Shōkō Asahara]].{{sfn|Anno|1997b|pp=93–96}} A book he edited, ''Schizo / Neon Genesis Evangelion'', describes her as an unsolvable puzzle; "a spirit that tears up the heart and body in eternity"; and "the most beautiful actress in the world"; it also characterizes her through a series of dichotomies, including opium and euphoria, [[Satan]] and God, Eros and [[death drive|Thanatos]], a cruel woman and a strong girl in junior high school, [[sarin]] for Gendo Ikari to destroy humanity and a keyhole to [[Pandora's box]], the [[Mother goddess|Great Bad Mother]] trying to take in her son, and hopelessness and eternal life.{{efn|Regarding the quoted material, Hideaki Anno originally wrote "{{lang|ja|永遠に心と軀を引き裂かれ続ける魂}}" and "{{lang|ja|世界で一番美しい女優}}", respectively.<ref>{{harvnb|Anno|1997a|pp=184–190}}</ref>}} Hiroki Azuma described Rei's solitude as conveying an extremely realistic image of the lack of communication experienced by contemporary children.<ref name=Azuma/> According to Azuma, Rei's inability to communicate typifies neither [[kogal|''kogyal'']], or "child girl", nor [[otaku]]. He also compared her room to Satyan, the scientific laboratory of Aum Shinrikyō.<ref name=Azuma/>

In the fourteenth episode of the anime, "Weaving a Story", Rei delivers a monologue about the world as she perceives it. During it, she asks herself "Who am I?" and addresses an unidentified interlocutor with "Who are you?"; the scene also shows multiple identical images of Rei arranged in a row. Dennis Redmond linked Ayanami's questions to "Who is No.1?", the refrain from ''[[The Prisoner]]'', and to the key line "Who am I?" from [[Heiner Müller]]'s ''Germania Death in Berlin''.{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=149}} According to Redmond, these multiple images recall the clones and androids common in 1970s science fiction, but the images appearing during Rei's monologue "suggest a spectrum from classic East Asian mythology", including the creation of [[Monkey King]] in ''[[Journey to the West]]'' from the union of Heaven and Earth, evoked by Rei through the words "sky" and "mountains".{{sfn|Redmond|2004|p=149}} Yuya Sato compared Rei's inner monologue to the introspective narration characteristic of girls' manga, or [[Shōjo manga|{{Transliteration|ja|shōjo}}]]. For example, [[Sakumi Yoshino]]'s manga ''Eccentrics'' features questions about personal identity and moments of introspection similar to Rei's "Who am I?" question. Sato argued that, as in {{Transliteration|ja|shōjo}} manga, Rei's question requires no definitive answer; its function lies in the act of questioning itself.{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|p=81}} Ayanami's stream of consciousness does not advance the plot; instead, she expresses her feelings chaotically through her dislikes and sympathies, a subjective world that Sato identified as another recurring theme of {{Transliteration|ja|shōjo}} manga.{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|p=83}} [[Gabriel Tsang|Gabriel F. Y. Tsang]] noted that Rei's monologue presents no God who has granted humans a purpose to survive, so "her enquiry about the nature of [[psychology of self|Self]] points to nothing".{{sfn|Tsang|2016|p=40}} Regarding the question "Who am I?", analyst Stevie Suan interpreted it as an expression of ''Evangelion''{{'}}s self-reflexivity and as a counterpoint to [[neoliberalist]] individualism.{{sfn|Suan|2021|p=246}}

Japanese architect Kaichiro Morikawa compared the distorted, deformed appearance of the first Rei to the installations of [[Tony Oursler]] and her bedroom to photographs by [[Gottfried Helnwein]].{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|p=27}} [[Kentaro Takekuma]] associated Rei's bandaged appearance with photographs by Romain Slocombe; Sadamoto compared her to the works of manga artist Sensha Yoshida.{{sfn|Anno|1997a|pp=170–172}} Throughout the series, images of the Moon frequently accompany Rei; the celestial body traditionally evokes motherhood, pallor, passivity, and femininity. Japanese engineer Yumiko Yano identified a hieratic, unattainable quality in Rei and compared her to the [[Virgin Mary]].{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|p=98}} Yano also linked Rei to the fragile, chaste women depicted in ''[[fin de siècle]]'' art, particularly in the works of [[Symbolism (movement)|Symbolist]] painters.{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|pp=101–102}} Critic Krystian Woznicki compared Rei's role to that of [[Pinocchio]] in the film ''[[964 Pinocchio]]'' (1991), but observed that "Rei's character is quite realistic, whereas ''Pinocchio'' is completely removed from reality".<ref name="Woznicki">{{cite magazine|last=Woznicki|first=Krystian|date=1997|title=Towards a Cartography of Japanese Anime: Hideaki Anno's "''Evangelion''"|magazine=Blimp Film Magazine|issue=36|pages=18–26|url=http://amsterdam.nettime.org/Lists-Archives/nettime-l-9802/msg00101.html|access-date=June 4, 2026|archive-date=October 23, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131023060845/http://amsterdam.nettime.org/Lists-Archives/nettime-l-9802/msg00101.html|url-status=live|location=Graz|publisher=GFW}}</ref> Saitō described her as "the culmination of the [[agalmatophilia|Pygmalionism]] that began with ''[[Nanako SOS]]''"{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=125}} and identified [[Ami Mizuno]] from ''Pretty Guardian Sailor Moon'' as a forerunner of Rei.{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=122}} Kenneth Lee likewise drew parallels between Rei's development of self-awareness and that of Pinocchio and Key from ''[[Key the Metal Idol]]''.<ref name=Lee>{{Cite web|last=Lee|first=Kenneth|date=September 9, 1998|title=The Thin Veneer Known as "''Evangelion''"|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/editorial/1998-09-09|url-status=live|access-date=February 27, 2021|website=[[Anime News Network]]|archive-date=July 24, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170724103237/http://www.animenewsnetwork.com/editorial/1998-09-09}}</ref> Patrick Drazen argued that Rei, similarly to other ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' characters, shares traits with deities from [[Shinto]] mythology, linking her to the Sun goddess [[Amaterasu]], who is reborn at every dawn. He similarly compared the show's two other protagonists, Shinji and Asuka, to [[Susanoo]] and [[Ama-no-Uzume]], respectively. Shinji, similarly to Susanoo, displays poor social skills and an unsociable disposition, and Asuka is ebullient and flaunts her body similarly to Uzume.{{sfn|Drazen|2014|pp=298–299}} According to Italian scholar Fabio Bartoli, Rei's three incarnations correspond to the three evolutionary stages of the [[soul]] postulated in the Jewish [[Qabbalah]]—''[[Nephesh]]'', mere animal vitality; ''[[Ruach]]'', the normal human soul; and ''[[Soul#Judaism|Neshamah]]'', the elevated spirit resulting from the connection between man and God.{{sfn|Bartoli|2008|pp=29–30}}

==Cultural impact==
===Reception===
Reception to Rei's character has been positive.<ref name="Anime Impact"/>{{sfn|Galbraith|2014|p=75}} Since 1995, fans have paid tribute to her through fan fiction, fan art, and [[doujinshi|{{Transliteration|ja|dōjinshi}}]].<ref name="SPH Magazines">{{cite magazine|title=Anime Fandom: Dealing In The Grey Areas|magazine=GameAxis Unwired|date=October 2007|issue=49|location=Singapore|publisher=[[SPH Magazines]]|page=80}}</ref><ref name=Raising>{{cite web|last=Cheung|first=Ever|url=http://www.animefringe.com/magazine/02.06/reviews/9/index.php3|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion: Ayanami Raising Project''|website=Animefringe|date=June 2002|access-date=April 20, 2020|archive-date=July 19, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110719060039/http://animefringe.com/magazine/02.06/reviews/9/index.php3|url-status=live}}</ref> Patrick W. Galbraith and Kathryn Hemmann have noted Rei's particular popularity among otaku, describing her as "the single most popular and influential character in the history of otaku anime".{{sfn|Hemmann|2020|pp=21–22}}<ref name="Post-Millennial">{{cite web|last=Galbraith|first=Patrick W.|url=http://www.japanesestudies.org.uk/articles/2009/Galbraith.html|title=Moe – Exploring Virtual Potential in Post-Millennial Japan|date=October 31, 2009|access-date=April 15, 2021|archive-date=October 21, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021030033/http://www.japanesestudies.org.uk/articles/2009/Galbraith.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hemmann further described Rei as representative of ''Evangelion'' itself.{{sfn|Hemmann|2014|p=49}} In September 2007, a column in ''[[The Nikkei]]'', published to coincide with the theatrical release of ''Evangelion 1.0'', estimated that at least one million men across Japan felt an attachment to Rei and described her as a modern goddess emblematic of Japanese anime.{{sfn|Galbraith|2022|p=239}}{{efn|The original quote in the column reads, "{{lang|ja|包帯姿で現れた現代の女神は日本アニメの申し子だ。}}" ["A modern goddess shown as a bandaged figure is the heaven-sent child of Japanese anime."]<ref name="Nikkei">{{Cite web|script-title=ja:春秋|trans-title=Spring and Autumn|url=http://www.nikkei.co.jp/news/shasetsu/20070831AS1K3100231082007.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070903120349/http://www.nikkei.co.jp/news/shasetsu/20070831AS1K3100231082007.html|archive-date=September 3, 2007|access-date=July 20, 2011|language=Japanese|website=Nikkei Net|publisher=[[The Nikkei]]|url-status=dead}}</ref>}} The column traced this attachment to the aftermath of [[Japanese asset price bubble|Japan's economic bubble]], when the collapse had left the country at a low point of self-confidence; it suggested that audiences identified with the wounded Rei silently and resolutely continuing to fight, and wondered whether a sense of emptiness had persisted despite the subsequent economic recovery.<ref name="Nikkei"/> According to scholar Akiko Sugawa-Shimada, ''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' characterized a 1990s fan practice of watching episodes as they communicate online, and fans discussed critical opinions and interest in female characters in ''Evangelion'', including Rei and Asuka Langley Soryu. The establishment of [[2-Channel]] and similar forums allowed fans to watch the anime and discuss it with others at the same time.  Similar fan activities later expanded globally with social networks such as X and YouTube.{{sfn|Sugawa-Shimada|2024|p=262}}

Anime critics described Rei as a realistic and influential character.<ref>Sources for critics' perceptions of Rei as realistic or influential:
* {{cite web|last=Azuma|first=Hiroki|author-link=Hiroki Azuma|title=Animé or Something Like it: ''Neon Genesis Evangelion''|url=http://www.ntticc.or.jp/pub/ic_mag/ic018/intercity/higashi_E.html|publisher=[[NTT InterCommunication Center]]|access-date=August 13, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120808182031/http://www.ntticc.or.jp/pub/ic_mag/ic018/intercity/higashi_E.html|archive-date=August 8, 2012}}
* {{Cite book|script-title=ja:新世紀エヴァンゲリオン(ニュータイプ100%コレクション)|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Kadokawa Shoten]]|year=2005|pages=24–25|language=ja|trans-title=Neon Genesis Evangelion Newtype 100% Collection|doi=10.11501/14577331|isbn=978-4-04-852700-2}}
* {{cite magazine|script-title=ja:今月の名場|trans-title=This Month's Famous Scenes|script-magazine=ja:アニメディア|trans-magazine=Animedia|date=February 1996|language=ja|location=Tokyo|publisher=[[Gakken]]}}
* {{cite web|last=Mackenzie|first=Chris|title=Top 25 Anime Characters of All Time|url=http://movies.ign.com/articles/103/1036651p4.html|website=[[IGN]]|access-date=October 22, 2009|date=October 20, 2009|archive-date=August 22, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110822171911/http://movies.ign.com/articles/103/1036651p4.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Tasha Robinson of ''[[Science Fiction Weekly]]'' praised the conclusion of Rei's character arc.<ref>{{cite web|last=Robinson|first=Tasha|access-date=April 14, 2021|url=http://www.scifi.com/sfw/anime/sfw3626.html|date=September 21, 1998|title=''Neon Genesis Evangelion'' 0:11–0:13|publisher=The Dominion|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090304071210/http://www.scifi.com/sfw/anime/sfw3626.html|archive-date=March 4, 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref> Chris Mackenzie of ''[[IGN]]'' distinguished Rei from the many similar characters that followed ''Evangelion'', arguing that she possessed greater inner depth than the characters modeled after her.<ref>{{cite web|last=Mackenzie|first=Chris|title=Top 25 Anime Characters of All Time|url=http://movies.ign.com/articles/103/1036651p4.html|website=[[IGN]]|access-date=October 22, 2009|date=October 20, 2009|archive-date=August 22, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110822171911/http://movies.ign.com/articles/103/1036651p4.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Other critics faulted her characterization as lacking personality, trite, or an unrealized opportunity.<ref>Sources for negative reception of Rei:
