url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325044946/https://www.avclub.com/evangelion-30-10-thrice-upon-a-time-review|archive-date=March 25, 2026|access-date=June 23, 2026|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|publisher=[[Paste Media Group]]}}</ref> Scholar Nicole Lamerichs noted the contrast between her life as a farmer and her former existence as an Evangelion pilot.{{sfn|Lamerichs|2022|p=88}} James Whitbrook of ''[[Gizmodo]]'' described the thoroughness of her character arc in ''3.0+1.0''.<ref>{{cite web|last=Whitbrook|first=James|url=https://gizmodo.com/evangelion-3-0-1-0-wouldnt-work-without-its-quiet-cont-1847646302|title=''Evangelion 3.0+1.0'' Wouldn't Work Without Its Quiet, Contemplative Opening|date=September 18, 2021|website=Gizmodo|access-date=November 27, 2021|quote=|archive-date=November 6, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211106014303/https://gizmodo.com/evangelion-3-0-1-0-wouldnt-work-without-its-quiet-cont-1847646302|url-status=live}}</ref> Chris Cimi of ''Otaquest'' similarly praised her development, describing her actions as something that "make for something different and warm but still ''Evangelion''".<ref>{{cite web|last=Cimi|first=Chris|date=March 9, 2021|title=''Evangelion 3.0+1.0'' Review (Spoilers): Hideaki Anno's Vision (Finally) Fulfilled|url=https://www.otaquest.com/evangelion-3-1-rebuild-fourth-anime-spoiler-movie-review/|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210812010418/https://www.otaquest.com/evangelion-3-1-rebuild-fourth-anime-spoiler-movie-review/|archive-date=August 12, 2021|access-date=November 27, 2021|website=Otaquest}}</ref>

===Merchandise===
[[File:Cosplayer of Rei Ayanami at FF29 20170211a.jpg|thumb|right|upright=0.7|alt=Cosplay of Rei Ayanami|The popularity of Rei Ayanami led to high demand for merchandise featuring her, resulting in high sales, speculation, and the creation of conventions dedicated to figure and fanzine markets.]]
Rei's popularity resulted in high demand for merchandise.{{sfn|Galbraith|2021a|p=100–101}} Merchandise featuring Rei includes figures, accessories, and a 2001 album titled ''Evangelion: The Birthday of Rei Ayanami''.<ref>Sources for Rei-themed merchandise:
* {{harvnb|Galbraith|2022|pp=241–242}}
* {{harvnb|Macias|Machiyama|2004|p=48}}
* {{harvnb|Li|2024|p=186}}
* {{harvnb|Lamarre|2009|p=205}}
* {{cite book|title=The Essential Evangelion Chronicle: Side A|date=2009|location=Grenoble|publisher=[[Glénat Editions]]|isbn=978-2-7234-7120-6|page=110}}</ref> Sadamoto's design for the character achieved considerable commercial success, driving strong figure sales and extensive speculation about Rei in the press and anime criticism.{{sfn|Lamarre|2009|p=205}} In Japan, sales of Rei merchandise contributed to the expansion of the figure and fanzine markets and encouraged the creation of dedicated conventions.<ref name="Struggle for Imagination"/><ref>{{cite web|last=Galbraith|first=Patrick W.|url=https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/moe/|title=Moe|website=Japanese Media and Popular Culture|publisher=[[University of Tokyo]]|date=April 11, 2020|access-date=November 2, 2021|archive-date=October 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019033937/https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/moe/|url-status=live}}</ref> According to one estimate, plastic models before ''Evangelion'' sold about 3,000 units; conversely, Rei's figures sold approximately ten times as many. ''Evangelion''{{'}}s release coincided with the early expansion of the Internet and a surge in anime figure sales, increasing the visibility of the otaku community;{{sfn|Galbraith|2010a|pp=215–216}} groups of otaku searching for Rei also began frequenting Akihabara, contributing to its transformation from an electronics district into a center of otaku culture.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=An instrument of change|magazine=[[Newtype USA]]|volume=5|issue=12|page=31|date=December 2006|location=Houston|publisher=[[A.D. Vision]]}}</ref>

In 2005, for the tenth anniversary of ''Evangelion''{{'}}s first broadcast, manga artist [[Mine Yoshizaki]] designed a series of anthropomorphic Angel action figures; among them, he created a figure of [[Angel (Neon Genesis Evangelion)#Lilith|Lilith]] based on Rei's character design.<ref>{{cite web|last=Macdonald|first=Christopher|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2005-11-01/lilith-angel-announced|title=Lilith Angel Announced|date=November 1, 2005|website=[[Anime News Network]]|access-date=April 13, 2020|archive-date=April 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412230137/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2005-11-01/lilith-angel-announced|url-status=live}}</ref> ''Evangelion'' models and toys initially recorded poor sales, but Rei's action figures quickly gained popularity and surpassed those of the Evangelion units, creating the first and perhaps the only case of a robotic anime "where reproductions of the human characters outsold those of the robots."{{sfn|Fujie|Foster|2004|p=98}} Books and magazines featuring Rei on their covers also achieved strong sales.{{sfn|Fujie|Foster|2004|p=39}} She also appeared on the cover of an issue of the Japanese edition of ''[[Rolling Stone]]''.<ref>{{cite web|last=Loo|first=Egan|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2007-08-11/evangelion-on-cover-of-rolling-stone-mag-in-japan|title=''Evangelion'' on Cover of ''Rolling Stone'' Mag...in Japan|date=August 11, 2007|website=[[Anime News Network]]|access-date=April 12, 2020|archive-date=May 8, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508150221/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2007-08-11/evangelion-on-cover-of-rolling-stone-mag-in-japan|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1997, Kadokawa Shoten published an art book devoted to the character, {{nihongo foot|''Evangelion Photograph – Rei''.|REI-レイ- 新世紀エヴァンゲリオン文庫写真集|Rei – Rei – Shinseiki Evangerion Bunko Shashin Shū|group=lower-alpha}}{{sfn|Hayashibara|1997}} Demand for Rei figures stimulated interest in sculpting representations of the character; her commercial presence established her as an example of a virtual idol.{{sfn|Galbraith|2021b|pp=77–78}} Rei also featured in promotional collaborations between ''Evangelion'' and other organizations, including Turner Colour; [[Honda]]; [[Nagoya]] and the [[Aonami Line]], as part of a campaign commemorating the release of ''Evangelion 3.0+1.0''; and the [[Japan National Tourism Organization]].<ref>Sources used for ''Evangelion'' collaborations:
* {{Cite web|last=Loveridge|first=Lynzee|date=July 28, 2015|title=Rei Ayanami Gets Official Blue Paint Color|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2015-07-28/rei-ayanami-gets-official-blue-paint-color/.91013|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150729220835/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2015-07-28/rei-ayanami-gets-official-blue-paint-color/.91013|archive-date=July 29, 2015|access-date=June 5, 2026|website=[[Anime News Network]]}}
