===Legacy===
Rei influenced Japanese animation and subsequent anime characters.<ref name="Struggle for Imagination"/> Following the popularity of ''Evangelion'', other characters sharing her aesthetic and personality traits emerged.<ref name="Shamoon"/><ref>{{cite web|last=Perdijk|first=Paul|url=https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/database-consumption/|title=Database Consumption|website=Japanese Media and Popular Culture|publisher=[[University of Tokyo]]|date=April 18, 2020|access-date=November 2, 2021|archive-date=October 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019173227/https://jmpc-utokyo.com/keyword/database-consumption/|url-status=live}}</ref> Critics regarded Rei's success as a catalyst for the [[Moe (slang)|{{Transliteration|ja|moe}}]] phenomenon.<ref name="Post-Millennial"/>{{sfn|Saikia|2021|p=262}} According to scholar Deborah Shamoon, much of ''Evangelion'''s cultural impact stemmed from Rei's {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} characteristics rather than its mecha and lore.<ref name="Shamoon">{{harvnb|Shamoon|2013|p=277}}</ref> Rei's injuries also played a crucial role in later events in the series and boosted sales of her merchandise.{{sfn|Li|2024|p=186}} Dolores Martinez said that Rei helped redefine the concept of {{Transliteration|ja|moe}}.{{sfn|Martinez|2015|p=79}} The rise of {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} encouraged the creation of characters built around specific stereotypical traits that the Japanese otaku audience could easily recognize and consume. In the late 1990s, characters bearing a close resemblance to Rei appeared on a massive scale in comics, anime, and novelizations, across both commercial and [[fanzine]] markets.{{sfn|Azuma|2009|pp=51–52}} According to Japanese scholar Hiroki Azuma in his book ''[[Otaku: Japan's Database Animals]]'', this proliferation did not result from direct imitation of ''Evangelion'', since "the emergence of Ayanami Rei did not influence many authors so much as change the rules of the {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} elements sustaining otaku culture".{{sfn|Azuma|2009|pp=51–52}} Authors working without ''Evangelion'' specifically in mind nevertheless created characters similar to Rei by combining recognizable {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} elements, such as a silent personality, blue hair, and an absence of emotions.{{sfn|Kacsuk|2021|p=122–124}}{{sfn|Denison|2022|p=111}} Azuma regarded the twenty-sixth episode, which depicts an alternate reality where Rei runs with a slice of bread in her mouth, as a turning point in otaku culture. For him, the scene marks the point where the era of "grand narratives" ends and the era of {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} characters begins. Thereafter, viewers' emotional attachment to characters assumed greater importance than a series's overarching narrative.{{sfn|Azuma|2007|p=182}}

Rei influenced the creation of Ruriko Tsukishima from ''[[Shizuku (video game)|Shizuku]]''.{{sfn|Azuma|2009|pp=49–50}} Both characters subsequently influenced [[Ruri Hoshino]] from ''[[Martian Successor Nadesico]]'', who in turn inspired the parodic Tsubame Ōtorii from ''[[Cyber Team in Akihabara]]''. Azuma argued that these characters share the same underlying information and cited them to illustrate his database hypothesis.{{sfn|Azuma|2012|pp=53–56}} Critics have also drawn comparisons between Rei and Anthy Himemiya in ''[[Revolutionary Girl Utena]]'',{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=125}} Diya in ''[[Butterfly Soup]]'',{{sfn|Fickle|Patterson|Han-Tani|Kim|2021|p=31}} Yashiro Kasumi in ''[[Muv-Luv]]'',{{sfn|Bruno|2019|pp=47–48}} [[Yuri (Doki Doki Literature Club!)|Yuri]] in ''[[Doki Doki Literature Club!]]'',{{sfn|Patterson|2022|p=30}} and the female cyborgs in ''[[Gunslinger Girl]]''.{{sfn|Ghilardi|2010|pp=119–120}} ''[[Serial Experiments Lain]]''{{'}}s Lain Iwakura has likewise been associated with Rei;{{sfn|Napier|2002|p=433}}{{sfn|Camp|Davis|2007|p=338}} ''Lain''{{'}}s principal screenwriter, [[Chiaki J. Konaka]], stated that ''Evangelion'' had not influenced the series and, although he appreciated both characters' traits, said he saw no similarities between them.<ref name="HK">{{cite magazine|date=April 2000|title=''Serial Experiments Lain''|magazine=[[HK Magazine]]|issue=14|publisher=Asia City Publishing|location=Hong Kong}} in {{cite web|url=http://www.konaka.com/alice6/lain/hk.html|title=HK Interview|publisher=[[Chiaki J. Konaka]]|access-date=September 25, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101124051110/http://konaka.com/alice6/lain/hk.html|archive-date=November 24, 2010|url-status=live}} and {{cite web|url=http://www.konaka.com/alice6/lain/hkint_e.html|title=HK Interview|publisher=[[Chiaki J. Konaka]]|access-date=September 25, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101101005437/http://konaka.com/alice6/lain/hkint_e.html|archive-date=November 1, 2010|url-status=live}}</ref> Conversely, Tamaki Saitō argued that Lain's decision to annihilate herself in ''Lain'' parallels Rei's decision in ''Evangelion''.{{sfn|Saito|Azuma|2011|p=125}} [[List of Eureka Seven characters#Eureka|Eureka]] from ''[[Eureka Seven]]'' has similarly been compared to Rei, a comparison that the series' screenwriter, [[Dai Satō]], expressed frustration with.<ref>{{cite web|last=Galbraith|first=Patrick W.|access-date=March 28, 2020|url=http://www.otaku2.com/articleView.php?item=679|title=Storywriter Sato Dai is frustrated with Japanese anime|date=2010b|website=Otaku2.com|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100727193900/http://www.otaku2.com/articleView.php?item=679|archive-date=July 27, 2010|url-status=live}}</ref> Critics Tomás Stiegwardt and Gabriel Los Santos compared [[Ahsoka Tano]] from ''[[Star Wars]]'' to Rei, describing both characters as reticent.{{sfn|Stiegwardt|Los Santos|2025|pp=69}} The modern female incarnation of [[Lu Meng]] in ''[[Ikki Tousen: Dragon Destiny|Dragon Destiny]]'' also draws her appearance from Rei's design.{{sfn|Ng|2013|p=240}}

Zoltan Kacsuk found that [[visual novel]]s were more likely than anime and manga to feature characters sharing {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} characteristics with figures such as Rei, and that such characters became especially common in the late 2000s and early 2010s.{{sfn|Kacsuk|2021|p=124–126}} Ryo Katsumata regarded Rei as {{nihongo foot|{{Transliteration|ja|mukuchi}},|無口|{{lit|mouthless}}|group=lower-alpha}} or reticent, and argued that reticence, blue hair, and eyepatches contributed to the development of new characters designed to appeal to otaku, including {{Transliteration|ja|moe}} characters.{{sfn|Katsumata|2020|pp=29–30}} Characters with her hairstyle frequently appeared in erotic comics shortly after ''Evangelion''{{'}}s success.{{sfn|Nagayama|2021|p=104}} The character also set an example for the {{Nihongo foot|[[kuudere|{{Transliteration|ja|kuudere}}]]|クーデレ|group=lower-alpha}} stereotype, a term for characters who hide their true feelings behind a melancholic and cold façade.{{sfn|Schrage|2025|p=173}}{{sfn|Raito Noberu Sakuhō Kenkyūsho|2012|p=228}} According to Kaichiro Morikawa, a Japanese architect and academic, characters with physical defects were rare before ''Evangelion'' and Rei; examples included [[Char Aznable]] in ''[[Mobile Suit Gundam]]'', who has a scar on his forehead, and Kushana in ''Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind''.{{sfn|Morikawa|1997|p=21}} The Japanese band [[Rey (band)|Rey]] derived its name from her.<ref>{{Cite web|date=November 25, 2008|script-title=ja:イケメンアニソンバンドがメジャーデビュー|trans-title=Ikemen Anison Band Has a Major Debut|url=http://www.oricon.co.jp/news/music/60445/full/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260701000648/https://www.oricon.co.jp/news/60445/full/|archive-date=July 1, 2026|access-date=September 20, 2013|website=Oricon Music|publisher=[[Oricon]]|language=ja}}</ref> Singer and guitarist Motoo Fujiwara wrote the lyrics to the song "[[Arue (song)|Arue]]", inspired by Rei; its English title, "R.A.", derives from her initials.<ref>{{Cite web|date=March 4, 2015|script-title=ja:バンプ・藤原基央が自身バンド以外初となる歌手・榎本くるみをプロデュース|trans-title=Bump's Motō Fujiwara Produces First Outside of Band with Kurumi Enomoto|url=https://www.oricon.co.jp/news/58028/full/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200413110851/https://www.oricon.co.jp/news/58028/full/|archive-date=April 13, 2020|access-date=April 15, 2020|website=Oricon Music|publisher=[[Oricon]]|language=ja}}</ref> During the [[Victoria's Secret Fashion Show 2012]], English model [[Jourdan Dunn]] wore an outfit resembling the character's plugsuit, the bodysuit worn by the Evangelion pilots; Gainax learned of the outfit only through the Internet and expressed confusion over the situation.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Dennison|first=Kara|date=December 18, 2020|title=Five Times ''Evangelion'' Escaped into Other Parts of Our Lives|url=https://otakuusamagazine.com/evangelion-references-in-other-shows/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201219133105/https://otakuusamagazine.com/evangelion-references-in-other-shows/|archive-date=December 19, 2020|access-date=June 3, 2026|website=[[Otaku USA]]|publisher=Sovereign Media}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Hodgkins|first=Crystalyn|url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2012-11-16/j-cast/gainax-not-contacted-about-victoria-secret-evangelion-lookalike|title=J-Cast: Gainax Not Contacted About Victoria Secret's ''Evangelion'' Lookalike|date=November 16, 2012|website=[[Anime News Network]]|access-date=April 12, 2020|archive-date=April 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412203029/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/interest/2012-11-16/j-cast/gainax-not-contacted-about-victoria-secret-evangelion-lookalike|url-status=live}}</ref> According to Monica Kim of [[Vogue (magazine)|''Vogue'']], her plugsuit also inspired a garment in the fashion house [[Louis Vuitton]]'s spring 2016 collection.<ref>{{cite web|last=Kim|first=Monica|url=https://www.vogue.com/article/louis-vuitton-spring-2016-inspiration-anime-evangelion-sailor-moon|title=The Secret Anime Muses Behind Louis Vuitton's New Collection: From ''Sailor Moon'' to ''Evangelion''|date=October 8, 2015|website=[[Vogue (magazine)|Vogue]]|access-date=April 15, 2020|archive-date=December 26, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191226134356/https://www.vogue.com/article/louis-vuitton-spring-2016-inspiration-anime-evangelion-sailor-moon}}</ref>
